---
/**
 * Fig. 1 (Video recorder) — where state lives and how the UI learns about changes.
 * The claim: Redis is the single ground truth shared by the C++ core and the Node
 * service, and the browser is pushed changes over SSE rather than polling for them.
 */
---

<figure>
  <div class="figbox">
    <svg
      viewBox="0 0 920 240"
      role="img"
      style="min-width:620px"
      aria-label="Camera streams feed a C++ pipeline built on ApraPipes, which writes device and stream state into Redis. A Node service reads and writes the same Redis state and pushes changes to the browser over server-sent events, so the interface is updated rather than polling."
    >
      <defs>
        <marker id="rs-ar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" fill="currentColor"></path>
        </marker>
        <marker id="rs-hl" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
          <path class="hlfill" d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z"></path>
        </marker>
      </defs>

      <g font-size="12" fill="currentColor">
        <text x="8" y="24" font-size="10.5" opacity="0.6">edge device (Jetson AGX / NX)</text>
        <rect x="8" y="34" width="440" height="128" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" opacity="0.4" stroke-dasharray="4 4"></rect>

        <rect x="30" y="70" width="110" height="56" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
        <text x="85" y="94" text-anchor="middle">Cameras</text>
        <text x="85" y="111" text-anchor="middle" opacity="0.62">streams</text>

        <rect x="196" y="70" width="132" height="56" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
        <text x="262" y="94" text-anchor="middle">C++ pipeline</text>
        <text x="262" y="111" text-anchor="middle" opacity="0.62">ApraPipes</text>

        <rect class="hl" x="470" y="62" width="118" height="72" fill="none" stroke-width="1.6"></rect>
        <text class="hl" x="529" y="90" text-anchor="middle">Redis</text>
        <text class="hl" x="529" y="107" text-anchor="middle" opacity="0.75">ground truth</text>

        <rect x="660" y="70" width="118" height="56" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
        <text x="719" y="94" text-anchor="middle">Node</text>
        <text x="719" y="111" text-anchor="middle" opacity="0.62">service</text>

        <rect x="836" y="70" width="76" height="56" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
        <text x="874" y="102" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text>

        <line x1="140" y1="98" x2="190" y2="98" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#rs-ar)"></line>

        <line class="hl" x1="328" y1="98" x2="464" y2="98" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#rs-hl)"></line>
        <text class="hl" x="396" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">writes state</text>

        <line class="hl" x1="654" y1="98" x2="594" y2="98" stroke-width="1.4" marker-end="url(#rs-hl)"></line>
        <text class="hl" x="624" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5">reads</text>

        <line x1="778" y1="98" x2="830" y2="98" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.2" marker-end="url(#rs-ar)"></line>
        <text x="804" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" opacity="0.72">SSE</text>
        <text x="804" y="132" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" opacity="0.55">push, not poll</text>

        <text x="529" y="176" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10.5" opacity="0.62">
          one store both processes agree on
        </text>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption>
    <b>Fig. 1 — One store both processes agree on.</b>
    Before this, the C++ core and the web layer each held their own idea of device state.
    Making Redis the single ground truth removed that disagreement, and server-sent events
    let the interface be told about changes instead of polling for them.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
